            <div class="clearfix text-formatted field field--name-field-body field--type-text-long field--label-hidden field__item"><h3><span lang="EN-US" dir="ltr"><strong>Visit the museum on a field trip (schools, summer camps, etc.)</strong></span><span>&nbsp;</span></h3>
<h4>Program description - Village Life</h4>
<p>Step into the past to better understand how people lived, worked and supported one another in rural Eastern Ontario during the 1920s and 1930s through an immersive, discovery-based experience at Cumberland Heritage Village Museum. Explore the site independently through interactive demonstrations, self-guided exploration and encounters with costumed villagers, learning by being in the spaces where history happened. Village Life offers a curriculum-based program for school groups from Kindergarten to Grade 6. The experience includes a tractor-pulled wagon ride (weather dependent), reserved picnic seating and a drink and snack for each child.&nbsp;</p>
<h4>Program delivery</h4>
<p>Cumberland Heritage Village Museum uses its historic buildings, landscapes and artefact collection to spark curiosity and meaningful connections to the past. Village Life emphasizes learning by doing, using stories, prompts and clues to encourage students to explore freely, ask questions of the villagers, including farmers and tradespeople, and draw their own conclusions based on observation and participation. Through these activities, students experience history as something lived and dynamic while having fun! &nbsp;</p>
<h4>Learning outcomes</h4>
<p>Students will identify changes and continuities between past and present community life and explore how daily activities were shaped by available technologies, resources and social relationships. Students will investigate characteristics of living things and natural resources, including plants and animals, and examine the ways people depended on and interacted with their environment. By inspecting buildings, tools, landscapes and village spaces, students will apply inquiry and observation skills to gather information and draw conclusions. Through scavenger hunts, conversations with villagers and immersive role-play, students will strengthen oral communication and active listening skills while developing historical imagination.&nbsp;</p>
<h4>Curriculum links</h4>
<ul>
<li><strong>Social Studies</strong>: Communities, heritage and identity, roles and responsibilities and how people lived and worked in the past and present.</li>
<li><strong>Science / STEM</strong>: Observation and inquiry; needs and characteristics of living things, everyday materials, tools and simple machines; growth and changes in plants, human interaction with the environment and natural resources.</li>
<li><strong>Language</strong>: Oral communication, listening and interpreting visual and material texts found on buildings, artefacts and printed materials.</li>
<li><strong>Arts</strong>: Drama and role-play; visual representation through drawing.&nbsp;</li>
</ul>
<h4>Availability, duration and cost</h4>
<p><strong>Availability: Mid-May to Thanksgiving. </strong>Visits can be booked to begin between 10 am and 3 pm. Teachers should expect to spend a minimum of 2 hours on site. The museum is open until 4 pm, however heritage demonstrations, wagon rides and canteen services are not available after 3 pm. &nbsp;</p>
<p><strong>Cost per child (2026): $6.57, taxes included. </strong>A one to seven (1:7) adult to child ratio is required. There is no charge for adult chaperones.&nbsp;</p>
<h4>Reservations</h4>
<p>Plan ahead and secure your field trip early! <a href="https://reservation.frontdesksuite.ca/rcfs/CumberlandHeritageVillageMuseum/Home/Index?pageId=4a800872-ed63-451a-8345-491f5d4c5061&amp;culture=en&amp;uiculture=en"><strong>Online booking form</strong></a><strong>.&nbsp;</strong>Your visit can be booked a maximum of 100 days in advance.&nbsp;<strong>By submitting the booking form, you confirm that you have read, acknowledged, and agreed to the information provided below ("Preparing your class," "Your health and safety" and "Other items to be aware of").</strong></p>
<p>After submitting the booking form, you will receive an automated confirmation email. If additional information is required to help prepare for your visit, a member of the museum team will follow up with you directly.</p>
<h4>Preparing your class</h4>
<p>Talk to your class about what you think you’ll see at the museum. Will there be farm animals, old cars or machines? Imagine what schools were like 100 years ago and whether all children attended. Think of questions to ask the blacksmith, tinsmith, printer, carpenter and leatherworker so you can learn about their trades. Be ready to spend lots of time outside, exploring the buildings, gardens and all the interesting corners of the museum. Remember to remind your students to stay with your group, watch their step and follow staff instructions onsite so everyone has a fun and safe visit.&nbsp;</p>
<h4><strong>Your health and safety</strong></h4>
<ol>
<li><span>Cumberland Heritage Village Museum is mainly an outdoor facility with hard packed gravel pathways between buildings. Visitors may encounter uneven ground. In winter, please wear proper footwear for snowy or icy conditions.</span></li>
<li><span>It is important to dress for the weather. Weather conditions might require the adjustment of activities, up to and including the cancellation of certain activities.</span></li>
<li><span>Please notify the museum of any allergy concerns prior to your visit.</span></li>
<li><span>If a member of your group gets separated, they should inform the closest member of staff that they cannot find their group.</span></li>
</ol>
<h4><strong>Other items to be aware of</strong></h4>
<ol>
<li><span>The heritage buildings on site were built to the standards of their time and some may not be accessible to all. If you have questions or concerns regarding accessibility,&nbsp;</span><a href="mailto:museecumberlandmuseum@ottawa.ca"><span>contact the museum </span></a><span>.</span></li>
<li><span>Please do not bring food or open drinks, other than water, into the buildings.</span></li>
<li><span>It is recommended not to drink water from the taps. The museum provides water coolers on site; please ensure everyone brings refillable water bottles.</span></li>
<li><span>The host for your visit will be bilingual and all handouts and signage are available in both English and French. However, we cannot guarantee that all experiences will be delivered in both languages.</span></li>
<li><span>Your host will greet you up to 5 minutes before your booking time. If you arrive before this time, please have the students wait on the bus and proceed to the train station (green and beige building by the parking lot) to inform&nbsp;staff that you have arrived. Please note that the building may not be open before 9 am.</span></li>
<li><span>If your group is larger than 120 students, please contact the museum&nbsp;<strong>before</strong>&nbsp;making your booking to ensure there is available capacity to accommodate your group.</span></li>
<li><span>Be sure to fill out the booking form as completely as possible. This ensures that the museum is prepared to give you the best possible experience.</span></li>
</ol>
<p>If you have any questions about the information on this page or about the visit, please <a href="mailto:museecumberlandmuseum@ottawa.ca">contact the&nbsp;museum</a>.</p></div>
      
